  "notes": "The record that invented \"stadium country\" — arena-rock dynamics and soft-rock harmony welded to a neotraditional band, windowed 1990–1997 because the research excludes the 1989 debut and calls No Fences \"the turning point.\" The harmony is the entry's most specific claim and is authored literally: \"the signature verse figure runs I – #Idim7 – ii7 – V(add9),\" so the top-weighted family is I–#Idim–ii–V with the sevenths carried in `extensions` rather than in the numerals, and the second family is the chorus reduction \"I–ii–V–I.\" Drums follow the stated grammar — cross-stick verses with the kick \"on 1 and the 'and-of-3'\" (`secondaryAnchor` 3&), choruses opening to 8th hats plus tambourine, and \"fills are minimal and pattern-preserving\" as an 8/16-bar fill spacing. The bass register is not inferred: the entry gives \"E1–D2, no glides,\" which is MIDI 28–38 with `glideProb` 0. ⚠ The catalogue tempo readings carry the research's own [LOW-CONF] class mark (the whole section's per-artist BPMs are cross-folded songdata/bpmdatabase values); the band is authored as the stated 66–115 spine and the small 150–170 uptempo group is deliberately left outside it. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity/humanize readings, the melodic densities and contour, and `countermelody.densityRatio` — \"note density low, long held notes at phrase ends\" and \"steel and fiddle trade the countermelody\" are directions, and 2–5, 0.5 and the arch weighting are this author's reading of them.",
